  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...The worst of the wind is behind us but windswept \nrain with minor coastal flooding/beach erosion and potential for \nsome localized urban and street flooding persists into Monday. \n\nMonitoring the progress of our Nor'easter on satellite we can see \nthe center of circulation already starting to move west into New \nJersey. It takes the core of the low level jet with it, thus, the \nworst of the wind is over with for southern New England. High Wind \nWarnings have been dropped as have Wind Advisories for eastern MA \nand RI while we'll continue those advisories for CT and \nwestern/central MA through the morning for any pockets of damaging \nwind we may see over the next several hours. Generally expecting NE \nwinds to continue to gust as high as 30-40 mph today. For the second \nhalf of the weekend we'll continue to deal with widespread, \nwindswept rainfall, heavy at times, which over the course of the \nnext two days will add up to 2-4 inches for most. Some locations may \nsee as much as 5-7 inches. For that reason, the Flood Watch \ncontinues through Monday though the long duration of the rainfall \nwill help to mitigate flooding issues. Turning attention to the \ncoast, we will continue the Coastal Flood Warning and Advisory for \nthe east and and south facing coasts respectively. As we've \nmentioned over the past several days, combination of the prolonged \nNE winds and high astronomical tides will contribute to coastal \nerosion and potential for minor coastal flooding through at least \nSunday, potentially into Monday. Fortunately both Nantucket and \nBoston managed to stay under flood stage with the overnight high \ntide, but will continue to approach flood stage over the next few \nhigh tide cycles along with locations up and down the coast. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Marked improvement Tuesday, followed by mainly dry \nand warmer  weather for the remainder of the week.\\\n\nBy Tuesday our low pressure system meanders its way offshore finally \nushering in winds from a different direction (namely, from the NW). \nAs the steering flow becomes more zonal going into the middle and \nlatter part of the week we'll see some movement and even some rising \nmid level heights. Along with a markedly drier pattern this allows \nfor a warming airmass with 850mb temps rising from around 10C to \nnear 15C by late week which spells a nice, if brief, warm-up back \ninto the mid/upper 70s late week before our next cold front brings \ncooler weather and perhaps some unsettled weather end of week.",
